17] [17592186480554 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186480491 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480554 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480554 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480554 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186480228 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480554 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "4bb5b3db-09a2-5068-870b-5e729655be69" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480578 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186480171 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480578 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186480395 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480578 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186480409 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480578 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186480440 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480578 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186480519 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480578 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480578 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480578 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186480171 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480578 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "86fb28cc-fb24-5ba3-a4c6-598034db31b6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480602 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186480117 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480602 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186480301 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480602 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186480311 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480602 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186480384 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480602 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186480517 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480602 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480602 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480602 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186480311 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480602 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "cd483cd6-8476-5430-add9-76a6040fadd1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480626 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186480148 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480626 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186480169 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480626 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186480177 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480626 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186480243 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480626 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186480248 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480626 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480626 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480626 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186480169 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480626 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "f8dc1988-bf2a-5231-bb4f-7e3b1ddd7888" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480634 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186480554 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480634 #edb/ref 1069 "q-97113" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480634 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480634 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\n[T]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,{P}_{B\\to C}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $[T]_{B}$ of a linear transformation $T$ with respect to the basis $B$ and the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{B\\to C}$ shown above. Find the matrix of $T$ relative to the basis $C.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480634 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "090d811d-2518-5f77-a8e6-7fb33e3ee0bb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480634 #edb/ref 1074 "The connection between the matrices of our linear transformation $T$ relative to the bases $B$ and $C$ is given by the following diagram:\n\n![](images/1b/1b81504d6fdcabee0e1f586fef3dd2af5a57a8411ba4d22429e00e09b171a4d1.png)\n\n \n\nHere, moving along an arrow means pre-multiplying by the corresponding matrix. Algebraically, we can write this as\n\n$$\n[T]_{C}={P}_{B\\to C}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}^{-1}\\,,\n$$\n\nwhere ${P}_{B\\to C}$ is the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C.$\n\nOur goal is to compute $[T]_{C}.$ First, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to C}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} & ={[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-2)\\cdot (-1)-3\\cdot 1}[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 \\\\ -3 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the matrix of $T$ relative to the basis $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T]_{C} & ={P}_{B\\to C}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-4 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & -4 \\\\ -14 & 11\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 5 & -3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480637 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186480602 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480637 #edb/ref 1069 "q-38361" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480637 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480637 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $[T]_{B}$ and $[T]_{C}$ be the matrices of a linear transformation $T$ relative to the bases $B$ and $C,$ respectively. If ${P}_{C\\to B}$ is the change-of-coordinates matrix from basis $C$ to basis $B,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $[T]_{B}=[T]_{C}^{-1}\\cdot {P}_{C\\to B}\\cdot [T]_{C}$\n\nII. $[T]_{B}={P}_{B\\to C}\\cdot [T]_{C}\\cdot {P}_{C\\to B}^{-1}$\n\nIII. $[T]_{C}={P}_{C\\to B}^{-1}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{C\\to B}$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480637 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "18c92889-12f7-533b-bf2f-74218efc0204" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480637 #edb/ref 1074 "The connection between the matrices of our linear transformation $T$ relative to the bases $B$ and $C$ is given by the following diagram:\n\n![](images/2e/2e82c232614bec8a98f079ea56d7913ee13e7b457e33e418198278d2e369fc5c.png)\n\nHere, moving along an arrow means pre-multiplying by the corresponding matrix. Algebraically, we can write this as\n\n$$\n[T]_{C}={P}_{C\\to B}^{-1}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{C\\to B},\n$$\n\nwhere ${P}_{C\\to B}$ is the change-of-coordinates matrix from $C$ to $B.$\n\nSolving this equation for $[T]_{B},$ we obtain the alternative formulation\n\n$$\n[T]_{B}={P}_{C\\to B}\\cdot [T]_{C}\\cdot {P}_{C\\to B}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480673 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186480578 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480673 #edb/ref 1069 "q-97132" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480673 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480673 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nB=\\{[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\},\\,[T]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the basis $B$ of ${R}^{2}$ and the matrix $[T]_{B}$ of a linear transformation $T$ with respect to this basis. Find the matrix of $T$ relative to the standard basis $S.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480673 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "79dda897-ef6c-5dc8-918f-90eef254a83f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480673 #edb/ref 1074 "The connection between the matrices of our linear transformation $T$ relative to the basis $B$ and standard basis $S$ is given by the following diagram:\n\n![](images/db/dbd020362f950fe8bfb4c592652b9e78a73610fa01a247b0b7ead6f201bf9ea5.png)\n\n \n\nHere, moving along an arrow means pre-multiplying by the corresponding matrix. Algebraically, we can write this as\n\n$$\n[T]_{S}={P}_{B\\to S}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to S}^{-1},\n$$\n\nand the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $S$ is\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to S}=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 7 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to S}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{B\\to S}^{-1} & ={[\\begin{aligned}3 & 7 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\cdot 3-1\\cdot 7}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -7 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 7 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the matrix of $T$ relative to the standard basis $S:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T]_{S} & ={P}_{B\\to S}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to S}^{-1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 7 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 & 7 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}16 & 41 \\\\ 5 & 12\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 & 7 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}9 & -11 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480696 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186480626 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480696 #edb/ref 1069 "q-140486" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480696 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480696 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nB=\\{[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\},\\,C=\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\},\\,[T]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the bases $B$ and $C$ of ${R}^{2},$ and the matrix $[T]_{B}$ of a linear transformation $T$ with respect to the basis $B$, shown above. Find the matrix of $T$ relative to the basis $C.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480696 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d84dee64-5460-51d7-b92e-0ebbf9e09851" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480696 #edb/ref 1074 "The connection between the matrices of our linear transformation $T$ relative to the bases $B$ and $C$ is given by the following diagram:\n\n![](images/1b/1b81504d6fdcabee0e1f586fef3dd2af5a57a8411ba4d22429e00e09b171a4d1.png)\n\n \n\nHere, moving along an arrow means pre-multiplying by the corresponding matrix. Algebraically, we can write this as\n\n$$\n[T]_{C}={P}_{B\\to C}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}^{-1},\n$$\n\nwhere ${P}_{B\\to C}$ is the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C.$\n\nFirst, let's denote the vectors of $B$ and $C$ as $\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}\\}$ and $\\{{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\}$, respectively.\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C$ is a matrix whose columns are the coordinates of vectors from $B$ relative to the basis $C.$\n\nHence, we need to find the coordinates of ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2}$ relative to the basis $C.$ To do this, we consider the matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | & | \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} & {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWe reduce $M$ using Gaussian elimination until we get the identity matrix on the left. By doing this, we obtain the required change-of-coordinates matrix on the right.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & -4 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 2 & -1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & -4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 6 & -2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & -4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 & -1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+3{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 5 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=[\\begin{aligned}5 & -2 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to C}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} & ={[\\begin{aligned}5 & -2 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5\\cdot (-1)-3\\cdot (-2)}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can compute the matrix of $T$ relative to the basis $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T]_{C} & ={P}_{B\\to C}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & -2 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}17 & -9 \\\\ 10 & -5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}10 & -11 \\\\ 5 & -5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
